# M16 docked at a 1920x1080 TV, off-screen: the Fit states where the Deck's own screen cannot show a difference.
#   SEMU_CHECK_REFRESH=60 SEMU_CHECK_SIZE=1920x1080 input-check.sh PAD m16-docked.cases OUT     (--plan accepts it too)
# SEMU_CHECK_REFRESH=60 is a 60 Hz TV (without it the private gamescope runs at the Deck screen's own refresh, 90 Hz
# on the OLED); a 120 Hz TV is SEMU_CHECK_REFRESH=120.
# The same format and evidence as m16-check.cases (a fresh OUT starts every system on its defaults); x,y are from the
# bottom left of 1920x1080, as surface0_content reads. The render scale's offered list stays the target's
# (visual.display 1280x800, a reversible decision in PLAN M16), so these cases press no Scale.
#
# K1. The PS1 TV (Beetle PSX, Crash Bandicoot). before: integer bezel, the picture 960x720 (3x) at 480,180 with the
#     whole TV on screen (3682cb6's fit drew the same). after-1 (81/2, FIT: NON-INTEGER GAME): 1440x1080 at 240,0.
#     after-2 (81/3, FIT: NON-INTEGER BEZEL): the whole TV as large as the screen holds. after-3 (81/0, FIT:
#     INTEGER GAME): 1280x960 (4x) at 320,60, the TV cut. after-4 (81/1): 960x720 again.
psx retroarch mednafen_psx 45 key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f -- Crash Bandicoot (USA).m3u
# K2. The PS2 (PCSX2, Def Jam): docked, integer bezel draws 1x, 597x448 at 661,316 with the whole TV, a quarter of the
#     area 3682cb6's fit drew (2x with the TV cut). after-1 (81/2): 1440x1080 at 240,0. after-2 (81/3): the whole TV
#     as large as the screen holds. after-3 (81/0, FIT: INTEGER GAME): 1195x896 (2x) at 363,92, the old docked size,
#     the TV cut by the screen. after-4 (81/1): 1x again. The owner rules whether a docked PS2 should start on
#     integer game (PLAN M16, item 5's docked sizes).
ps2 pcsx2 - 60 key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f -- Def Jam - Fight for NY (USA).iso
# K3. The NES TV (Mesen, Super Mario Bros. 3): integer bezel draws 2x, 640x480 at 640,300 with the whole TV; 3682cb6's
#     fit drew 3x (960x720) with the stand 59 px past the bottom, 5.5% of the 1076-row set, past the owner's 1% (a
#     picture centred down the screen leaves the set no room at 3x). after-1 (81/2): 1440x1080 at 240,0. after-3
#     (81/0, FIT: INTEGER GAME): 1280x960 (4x) at 320,60. The same holds for the black NES TV and the speakers TVs of
#     the PS1 (2x, 640x480; 3x left 3.3% past) and N64 (626x474 against 939x711; 2.6% past).
nes retroarch mesen 20 key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f -- Super Mario Bros. 3 (USA) (Rev 1).zip
# K4. The Wii U (Cemu, Mario Kart 8), no bezel: Fit flips integer game and non-integer game. before: 1280x720 at
#     320,180 on black (1x of the game's 720 lines). after-1 (81/1, FIT: NON-INTEGER GAME; with no bezel the Fit
#     list is integer game and non-integer game): 1920x1080 at 0,0. On the Deck's 800 rows both states are
#     1280x720, so only a docked screen shows the difference.
wiiu cemu - 60 key:ctrl+shift+f -- MARIO KART 8 (US).wua
# K5. Item 8 docked: the Dreamcast (Flycast, Sonic Adventure) at a 60 Hz TV, no input, on its defaults (the TV,
#     GDV-NTSC (Dreamcast) since M16 item 9, integer bezel: the 640x480 picture at 1x, at 640,300, with the whole TV;
#     pitch "scanlines 1 rows each, intres 0.000 (480 lines drawn 480 tall)"). rate: the Sega logo at least 59.8
#     per second with the longest gap under 25 ms, then the opening movie, which the game draws at 30 frames a second,
#     at least 29.8 per second with the longest gap under 50 ms (one and a half of its frames). At a whole multiple of
#     60 Hz the swap-interval patch leaves Flycast's own interval, so its frames come evenly (the logo's about 17 ms
#     apart, the movie's about 34 ms; off-screen on 2026-10-06 with vsync off instead they bunched to 22 and 44 ms).
#     before: the movie inside the whole TV.
dreamcast flycast - 90 -- Sonic Adventure (USA) (En,Ja,Fr,De,Es) (Rev A).chd
